course: dictionary
lesson: 13491
title: Ignorant: three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, so context tells you which one to use. «Ella was ignorant of the new rules, so she broke one by accident.» Here ignorant means she did not know about something. Read the words around it and you can see that she simply had no information.
explain: «He was ignorant about how plants grow, but he asked lots of questions.» This means he had little knowledge in general, and he wanted to learn. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: In UK and Irish speech, ignorant can be informal and mean rude. «Don't be so ignorant, you didn't even say hello!» The speaker is not saying the person lacks knowledge. They are saying the person was bad-mannered.
explain: Be careful with this word. Calling someone ignorant can be insulting, so think before you use it about a person. Notice the phrases too: ignorant of, an ignorant comment, and wilfully ignorant, which means choosing not to know.
fact: Ignorant means not knowing about something, or having no information about it.
fact: Ignorant can also mean without much education or knowledge in general.
fact: In UK and Irish informal speech, ignorant can mean rude or bad-mannered.
fact: «Ignorant of» is followed by the thing you do not know, as in «ignorant of the rules».
fact: Calling someone ignorant can be insulting, so use it carefully.
exercise: «The traveller was ignorant of the local customs.» What does ignorant mean here? | Very rude and unfriendly | Lost in the fog | * Not knowing about something | Very clever
exercise: Mia says, «Don't be so ignorant, you didn't say hello!» What does ignorant mean here? | Not knowing anything at all | Without much education | Shy and quiet | * Rude or bad-mannered
exercise: «He was ignorant of the rules, so he made a mistake.» Find and copy the word that tells you he did not know. | he | * ignorant | rules | mistake
test:
Why might it be unkind to call someone ignorant? | Because it can be insulting | Because it means they are tall | * Because it can be insulting and hurt their feelings | Because it means they are happy
test:
«The ignorant comment hurt her feelings.» What is an ignorant comment? | A comment about the weather | A song that is happy | * A comment that shows a lack of knowledge, or is rude | A comment in a language she knows
exam:
«She was ignorant of the way to the station, so she took a wrong turn.» What does ignorant mean here? | * Did not know about something | Very rude and unfriendly | Very clever at maps | Happy to be lost
